  "worked_solution": "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-9}-\\frac{1}{x+5} & =\\frac{x(x+5)-({x}^{2}-9)}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x-{x}^{2}+9}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x+9}{({x}^{2}-9)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x+9}{(x-3)(x+3)(x+5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\n(x-3)(x+3)(x+5)=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=-5\\,,\\,x=-3\\,,\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=-5$,  $x=-3,$ and $x=3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
